\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{An edge derivative is half its variance}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the [induced action](fdip-005R), with #{m>0} fixed,
##{\frac{\partial\mathcal W}{\partial w_e}
 =\frac12 d_e^TGd_e
 =\frac12\mathbb E[(\phi_{h(e)}-\phi_{s(e)})^2],}
where #{\phi\sim N(0,G)}. Each derivative is nonnegative.}

\proof{
\p{For any square matrix #{B}, multilinearity of the determinant
gives #{\det(I+tB)=1+t\operatorname{tr}B+O(t^2)}: the linear
terms select one diagonal entry of #{tB}, and all higher terms use
at least two entries of #{tB}. Factoring an invertible #{Q} yields
##{\det(Q+tA)=\det Q\det(I+tQ^{-1}A).}
When #{Q>0} and #{A} is symmetric, the perturbed matrix is positive
for sufficiently small #{|t|}. Differentiating its real logarithm
therefore gives
##{\left.\frac{d}{dt}\log\det(Q+tA)\right|_{t=0}
 =\operatorname{tr}(Q^{-1}A).}
Set #{A=d_ed_e^T}; cyclicity of trace gives
#{\operatorname{tr}(Gd_ed_e^T)=d_e^TGd_e}. Finally the
[covariance identity](fdip-000B) says
#{\mathbb E[(d_e^T\phi)^2]=d_e^TGd_e\ge0}.}
}

\p{The matrix differential is the log-determinant gradient in
\citet{Appendix A.4.1, pp. 641-642}{boyd2004convex}.
TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{Matrix.fderiv_det_apply} states
#{D\det(Q)[A]=\det(Q)\operatorname{tr}(Q^{-1}A)} for a finite
matrix over a complete nontrivially normed field whose determinant
is a unit. Over #{\mathbb R}, positivity supplies this hypothesis.
It supports the determinant derivative; the logarithm, edge direction
and Gaussian interpretation are the steps proved here.}
